<%- include('partials/head') %>

<div class="page-head">
  <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
  <div class="meta">What BotApprove stores, why, and for how long</div>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <p class="updated">Last updated <%= updated %>. Operated by <%= operator %>.</p>

  <p>
    BotApprove is a security tool, so it deliberately keeps a record of what it did and why. This
    page lists exactly what is stored. It is written against the actual database schema rather
    than in general terms.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stored</h2>

  <h3>About servers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Server ID and name.</li>
    <li>Your settings: approval channel, log channel, nickname, low-resolution threshold, keyword
      blocklist, approver role IDs, and the nuke inviter action.</li>
    <li>Approved bot IDs, and who approved each one.</li>
    <li>Subscription state, and the SHA-256 hash of any licence key redeemed. Plaintext keys are
      never stored.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>About people</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Discord user IDs</strong> of people who approve or deny a bot, change settings,
      submit to the threat list, or invite a bot that gets screened.</li>
    <li>The username of a person named in a nuke bot incident, so the server owner knows who to
      look at.</li>
    <li>Your Discord ID, username and avatar hash when you sign in to the dashboard, held in a
      server-side session.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Some of this is about people who never used the dashboard themselves. If someone invites a
      bot to a server running BotApprove, their user ID is recorded in that server's audit trail.
      That is the point of the tool: a server owner needs to know who let a bot in.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>About bots</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Bot user IDs, tags, account creation dates, and the permissions their role held on join.</li>
    <li>Avatar and banner dimensions when the image check runs. The images themselves are used to
      build the approval message and are not kept afterwards.</li>
    <li>Threat list entries and the reason given for each.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Security log</h3>
  <p>
    Every kick, approval, denial, ban, token issue, settings change and permission alert is
    written to a durable log with a timestamp, the server, the bot, and the acting user ID. This
    is the audit trail, and it is the part of the system that a server owner relies on after an
    incident.
  </p>

  <h2>What is not stored</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Message content.</strong> BotApprove does not request the Message Content intent
      and cannot read your messages.</li>
    <li><strong>Member lists</strong>, beyond the specific users named above.</li>
    <li><strong>Voice data</strong>, presence, or activity.</li>
    <li><strong>Your Discord OAuth token.</strong> It is used once at sign-in to read your identity
      and server list, then discarded. It is never written to disk.</li>
    <li>Payment card details. Any payment is handled by a payment provider, not by this service.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it is stored</h2>
  <p>
    To operate the approval gate you asked for, to give server owners an audit trail of security
    decisions, to enforce subscriptions, and to review threat list submissions. Where a lawful
    basis is required, it is legitimate interest in securing a Discord server, and contract
    performance for paid features.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is shared with</h2>
  <p>
    Nobody. Data is not sold, rented, or given to advertisers or analytics services. There are no
    third-party trackers on this site. The only outbound requests the dashboard makes are to
    Discord's CDN for server icons, GitHub for the maintainer's avatar, and Google Fonts for the
    typeface.
  </p>
  <p>
    One deliberate exception: if the operator approves a threat list submission, the bot ID and the
    reason given become visible to every server running BotApprove. The submitting server and user
    are not published.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it lives</h2>
  <p>
    In a SQLite database on a private server, reachable only over an encrypted connection. Sessions
    are stored server-side so signing out actually invalidates them.
  </p>

  <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Settings, whitelist and keywords:</strong> until you change them, or you ask for
      the server's data to be deleted. They are kept when the bot is removed, so re-adding it
      restores your configuration.</li>
    <li><strong>Security log and incidents:</strong> kept as the audit trail. Deleted on request.</li>
    <li><strong>Re-invite tokens:</strong> minutes. Deleted the moment they are used or expire.</li>
    <li><strong>Sessions:</strong> up to 7 days, or until you sign out.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    You can ask what is held about you, ask for it to be corrected, or ask for it to be deleted.
    Depending on where you live you may also have the right to object to processing, or to
    complain to a data protection authority. Requests go through the contact link below.
  </p>
  <p>
    A server owner can have their whole server's record erased. Where deleting a specific entry
    would break the audit trail another server depends on, that will be explained rather than
    silently refused.
  </p>

  <h3>Automated decisions</h3>
  <p>
    If a server enables it, BotApprove can automatically kick or ban the account that invited a
    bot on the threat list. That decision is made by software, and the server owner is sent a
    notification with buttons to reverse it. If you were affected and want it reviewed, contact the
    owner of that server first, since they control the setting and the ban.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    The service is not directed at anyone below Discord's minimum age for their country. If you
    believe a child's data is held here, get in touch and it will be removed.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    This policy may change. Material changes will be noted by the date at the top of this page.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Privacy requests go through
    <a href="<%= contactUrl %>" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</div>

<%- include('partials/foot') %>
